---
description: Fix root causes; never hide broken behavior behind hacks or silent workarounds.
alwaysApply: true
---

# Root-cause fixes

- When behavior fails, reproduce it and identify the failing layer before changing
  the implementation. Do not add hacks to make the failure appear resolved.
- Fix the underlying implementation. If the defect is in a dependency, prefer an
  upstream fix or a verified upstream release that resolves it.
- Do not silently substitute different semantics, suppress meaningful errors or
  exit status, bypass validation, fabricate success, or add compatibility branches
  to conceal a defect. Do not weaken tests to obtain a passing result.
- If a proper fix is unavailable or outside the authorized scope, report the
  limitation and supporting evidence explicitly. Leave it visible rather than
  introducing an unrequested workaround.
- Keep necessary recovery, rollback, protocol handling, and supported platform
  behavior explicit. These must preserve correctness and report unresolved
  failures; they are not permission to hide errors.
- Tests and test doubles must follow the production contract. Do not weaken
  production behavior merely to accommodate incomplete test doubles.
- Respect the existing unreleased-development policy: do not introduce legacy
  compatibility or migrations without an explicit requirement.
